"HiBy R3II(2025) User Manual | HiBy WiKi (https://guide.hiby.com/en/docs/products/audio_player/hiby_r3ii_2025/guide/)\nciteturn823view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://guide.hiby.com/en/docs/products/audio_player/hiby_r3ii_2025/guide/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 671\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2:   * 【1†Player】\nL3: \nL4:     * 【2†HiBy RS2II】\nL5: \nL6:     * 【3†HiBy RS8II】\nL7: \nL8:     * 【4†星海贝 M500MIKU】\nL9: \nL10:     * 【5†HiBy R4MIDI】\nL11: \nL12:     * 【6†HiBy R1MIDI】\nL13: \nL14:     * 【7†HiBy R6ProII(2025)】\nL15: \nL16:     * 【8†HiBy R3PROII】\nL17: \nL18:     * 【9†HiBy R6ProMax Titanium】\nL19: \nL20:     * 【10†HiBy R6III(2025)】\nL21: \nL22:     * 【11†HiBy R6ProMax】\nL23: \nL24:     * 【12†HiBy R3II(2025)】\nL25: \nL26:       * 【12†HiBy R3II(2025) User Manual】\nL27:       * 【13†HiBy R3II(2025) Firmware】\nL28:       * 【14†HiBy R3II(2025) Spec】\nL29:     * 【15†HiBy R1】\nL30: \nL31:     * 【16†HiBy R4 EVA】\nL32: \nL33:     * 【17†HiBy R4】\nL34:     * 【18†HiBy R8II】\nL35: \nL36:     * 【19†星海贝 M300】\nL37: \nL38:     * 【20†HiBy R3II】\nL39: \nL40:     * 【21†HiBy R6ProII】\nL41: \nL42:     * 【22†HiBy R6III】\nL43: \nL44:     * 【23†HiBy R2II】\nL45: \nL46:     * 【24†HiBy RS8】\nL47: \nL48:     * 【25†HiBy RS2】\nL49: \nL50:     * 【26†HiBy RS6】\nL51: \nL52:     * 【27†HiBy R3Pro】\nL53: \nL54:     * 【28†HiBy R3Pro Saber】\nL55: \nL56:     * 【29†HiBy R6PRO】\nL57: \nL58:     * 【30†HiBy R6】\nL59: \nL60:   * 【31†USB DAC】\nL61: \nL62:   * 【32†HiFi Earphone】\nL63: \nL64:   * 【33†BT Headphone/BT Reciver】\nL65: \nL66:   * 【34†Dock】\nL67: \nL68:   * 【35†HiFi周边】\nL69: \nL70:   * 【36†HiBy & HiBy Digital Comparison】\nL71: \nL72: [Button: On this page]\nL73: # HiBy R3II(2025) User Manual\nL74: \nL75: Welcome to use HiBy's products\nL76: \nL77: Thank you for purchasing the portable high-definition lossless music player R3II (2025) launched by HiBy. In order to make R3II(2025) bring you a better experience, this article introduces the common functions and operating methods of R3II(2025), hoping to help you understand and use R3II(2025).\nL78: At the same time, you are also welcome to log in to the official website of HiBy at 【37†https://store.hiby.com/†store.hiby.com】 to learn more about HiBy product information and purchase related products.\nL79: \nL80: R3II 2025 Product Picture\nL81: \nL82: 【38†Image: R3II 2025 Product Picture】 【39†Image: R3II 2025 Product Picture】\nL83: ## 1. Product introduction【40†​】\nL84: \nL85: R3II(2025) is a portable HiFi HD lossless music player\nL86:   1. Based on HiBy OS (Linux 4.4.94) system, the Junzheng X1600E main control is adopted\nL87:   2. Adopt dual CS43198 decoding architecture, with maximum support for DSD256\\PCM384kHz decoding\nL88:   3. Supports analog outputs such as 4.4mm Balance, 3.5mm PhoneOut(PO) and other analog outputs\nL89:   4. Supports coaxial (S/PDIF) output\nL90:   5. Supports Bluetooth audio output (supports LDAC, aptX, AAC, SBC, etc. encoding)\nL91:   6. Supports USB audio (USB Audio) output\nL92:   7. Supports external MicroSD(TF) cards up to 2TB\nL93:   8. Supports Bluetooth 5.0 and 2.4G WiFi\nL94:   9. Support local playback, AirPlay, DLNA, network radio, etc.\nL95:   10. Supports inputs such as Bluetooth, USB Audio, etc.\nL96: appearance introduction\nL97: \nL98: The following introduction is the front view of the player screen as reference:\nL99: \nL100:   1. From top to bottom on the right are: Power button (LED indicator), Previous song, Playback Pause, Next song\nL101:   2. From top to bottom on the left are: lanyard interface, volume up, volume down, MicroSD (TF) card slot\nL102:   3. The bottom is from left to right: BAL, Type-C, PO\nL103: ## 2. Button usage instructions【41†​】\nL104: \nL105: Button distribution\nL106: \nL107: The following introduction is the front view of the player screen as reference:\nL108: \nL109:   1. From top to bottom on the right are: Power button (LED indicator), Previous song, Playback Pause, Next song\nL110:   2. From top to bottom on the left are: lanyard interface, volume up, volume down, MicroSD (TF) card slot\nL111: Button  | Function\nL112: --- | ---\nL113: Power button  | Long press: power switch; short press: switch screen; long press for 10s or more: hardware reset\nL114: Volume +  | Short press: Increase the volume\nL115: Volume -  | Short press: reduce the volume\nL116: Play Pause  | Short button: Play Pause\nL117: Next song  | Short button: Next song\nL118: Press and hold the next song to turn on  | Used to enter the burn mode, mainly for production\nL119: Press and hold play to pause the power on  | Used to force the upgrade mode, see the specific operation`upgrade mode`\nL120: [upgrade mode]\nL121: \nL122:   1. Make sure there is a MicroSD(TF) card inserted\nL123:   2. Make sure there is an upt file for upgrade in the inserted MicroSD(TF) card\nL124:   3. The system detects the upgrade file and will automatically upgrade it.\nL125: ## 3. Interface usage instructions【42†​】\nL126: \nL127: Interface Distribution\nL128: \nL129: The following introduction is the front view of the player screen as reference:\nL130: \nL131:   1. From top to bottom on the left are: lanyard interface, volume up, volume down, MicroSD (TF) card slot\nL132:   2. The bottom is from left to right: BAL, Type-C, PO\nL133: ### Audio output interface【43†​】\nL134: \nL135: audio output interface\nL136: \nL137: The audio output interface can be provided to different audio devices for use, such as headphones with 3.5mm interface, etc.\nL138: \nL139:   1. PO: 3.5mm single-ended interface headphone output\nL140:   2. BAL: 4.4mm balanced interface headphone output\nL141: ### USB port【44†​】\nL142: \nL143: The physical interface used by the USB interface is the Type-C interface, through which the following functions can be provided. The player allows USB to work in two different modes:`USB OTG`and`USB Device`。\nL144: \nL145: USB OTG\nL146: \nL147: When the player as`USB OTG`When using it (can be understood as USB on the computer):\nL148: \nL149:   1. Connecting a USB or mobile hard drive: Read data from a USB or mobile hard drive;\nL150:   2. Connect to a USB decoder or USB small tail: output high-definition audio data;\nL151: \nL152: USB device\nL153: When the player as`USB Device`When using:\nL154: \nL155:   1. USB MassStorage (USB file management), some questions about the use of USB MassStorage can be found【45†Here】 。\nL156:   2. USB audio, some questions about USB audio usage can be found【46†Here】 。\nL157: ### Charging interface【47†​】\nL158: \nL159: charging interface\nL160: \nL161: The physical interface used by the charging interface is the Type-C interface, through which the charger can be connected to the charger.\nL162: \nL163:   1. Charging supports ordinary charging heads, which can reach a charging current of 5V@1A.\nL164: ### MicroSD(TF) card interface【48†​】\nL165: \nL166: MicroSD(TF) card interface\nL167: \nL168: The MicroSD(TF) card interface is used to insert a MicroSD(TF) card, mainly to expand storage capacity. The HiBy player can support MicroSD(TF) cards up to 2TB, and the player supports file systems including FAT32\\exFAT\\NTFS.\nL169: ### LED Indicator【49†​】\nL170: \nL171: Status  | Color\nL172: --- | ---\nL173: Normal  | Light blue ■\nL174: Charging  | Red breathing effect (normal)■\nL175: PCM(~48k)  | Yellow■\nL176: PCM(88.2k~192k)  | Cyan■\nL177: PCM(352.8k~)  | Orange■\nL178: DSD  | White ■\nL179: MQA  | Green■\nL180: MQA Studio  | Blue■\nL181: MQA Core (MQB)  | Magenta■\nL182: \nL183: ## 4. Get started【50†​】\nL184: \nL185: Get Started\nL186: \nL187: Since the player does not have storage itself, the user needs to prepare a MicroSD(TF) card, or you can purchase a package product with a MicroSD(TF) card.\nL188: ### First boot【51†​】\nL189: \nL190: first boot\nL191: \nL192:   1. Press and hold the boot key for 2s, and the player enters the boot state\nL193:   2. After the startup animation is completed, the system enters the boot settings interface\nL194:   3. Select the corresponding language\nL195:   4. Complete the setup\nL196:   5. The first startup is completed\nL197: ### Status bar【52†​】\nL198: \nL199: status bar\nL200: \nL201: At the top is some information in the status bar, showing some common system information.\nL202: \nL203:   1. Volume value Display the volume value of the current output port. The volume value in red indicates that the current volume value is too high. Users need to be careful not to use it for a long time at high volume, otherwise it may cause hearing loss.\nL204: \nL205:   2. Output port status LineOut PhoneOut Balance Balance LineOut\nL206:   3. Play status If it is currently in playback, the playback status or the pause status will be displayed, otherwise it will not be displayed.\nL207: \nL208:   4. time Show current time\nL209: \nL210:   5. Bluetooth status information Displays Bluetooth information, including signal strength, connection status, etc.\nL211: \nL212:   6. WiFi status information Displays WiFi information, including signal strength, connection status, etc.\nL213:   7. Battery level information Displays battery icon, charging status, percentage and other information. Power percentage switch: Settings -》 Battery -》 Battery percentage.\nL214: \nL215:   8. HiByLink Status Information Showcases HiByLink connection information.\nL216: ### Main interface【53†​】\nL217: \nL218: Introduce the relevant functions of the main interface and some common operations. The main interface contains a series of entrances to quickly use related functions.\nL219: \nL220: gesture operation\nL221: \nL222:   1. Swipe left Quickly enter the playback interface.\nL223: \nL224:   2. Swipe down from outside the screen Enter the quick operation interface.\nL225: \nL226: main interface\nL227: \nL228:   1. music\nL229:   2. Streaming media\nL230:   3. wireless\nL231:   4. E-book\nL232:   5. USB audio\nL233:   6. System\nL234: ### Quick operation【54†​】\nL235: \nL236: gesture operation\nL237: \nL238:   1. Any interface, swipe down from outside the screen to enter the quick operation interface.\nL239:   2. Follow the following operations: System -》 Shortcut menu, you can enter the shortcut menu setting interface\nL240:      * Modify quick operation pull-up trigger and pull-down trigger\nL241:      * Modify the default operation of the quick operation and place the quick operation that is more suitable for you in this position\nL242: \nL243: Operation introduction\nL244: The following shortcut buttons are included by default: Bluetooth, WiFi, Sleep Shutdown, Gain, Brightness Adjustment, and the currently playing song name and media buttons. You can use the following operations: System -》 Shortcut menu, you can enter the shortcut menu settings, and then replace the shortcut action with the shortcut action supported by other systems.\nL245: \nL246:   1. Bluetooth Click the Bluetooth icon and switch Bluetooth. Press and hold the Bluetooth icon to enter the Bluetooth settings interface.\nL247:   2. WiFi Click the WiFi icon toggle on and on. Press and hold the WiFi icon to enter the WiFi settings interface.\nL248: \nL249:   3. DLNA Click the DLNA icon to switch DLNA.\nL250: \nL251:   4. AirPlay Click the AirPlay icon toggle on and on AirPlay.\nL252: \nL253:   5. HiByLink Click the HiByLink icon to switch HiByLink.\nL254: \nL255:   6. MSEB Click the MSEB icon, switch MSEB Press and hold the MSEB icon to enter the MSEB settings interface.\nL256: \nL257:   7. Output selection Click the [Output Selection] icon to switch PO/LO\nL258:   8. Gain settings Click the gain icon to adjust the gain.\nL259: \nL260:   9. Fade in and out Click the fade icon to fade in and fade in and out\nL261: \nL262:   10. Gapless playback Click the seamless play icon, switch play seamlessly\nL263: \nL264:   11. Sleep shutdown Click the Sleep Shutdown icon toggle on and off, and start countdown synchronously. If you need to set different times of sleep shutdown, you need to enter the System -> Sleep shutdown adjustment.\nL265: ## 5. Music【55†​】\nL266: \nL267: ### Music interface【56†​】\nL268: \nL269:   1. The tops are: return to the previous interface, music search, collection classification, playback settings\nL270:   2. Click the corresponding icon to enter the corresponding function.\nL271:   3. Music module, including a list entry according to songs, folders, albums, singers, styles, album singers, etc.;\nL272: ### Import local music【57†​】\nL273: \nL274: [Import local music]\nL275: \nL276: Since the player itself does not have storage, the user needs to prepare a MicroSD(TF) card, or you can purchase a package product with a MicroSD(TF) card.\nL277: \nL278: #### Import music via MicroSD(TF) card【58†​】\nL279: \nL280: MicroSD(TF) card\nL281: \nL282: Insert the MicroSD(TF) card containing the song into the player's MicroSD(TF) card slot. The player supports FAT32\\exFAT\\NTFS and other file formats.\nL283: #### Import music via USB【59†​】\nL284: \nL285: The player supports connecting to the computer via USB and then using the MassStorage protocol to transfer files. Here is a brief introduction. For more information about the use of MassStorage, please refer to【45†Here】 。\nL286: The following is`MacOS`and`Windows`Make a brief description:\nL287: \nL288: MacOS\nL289: MacOS supports FAT and exFAT, but does not support NTFS. Therefore, if you are using a NTFS file system, you need to install a third-party driver to use it. The following two methods are available:\nL290: \nL291: app\nL292: \nL293:   * Paragon：【60†https://www.paragon-software.com/home/ntfs-mac//†www.paragon-software.com】\nL294:   * Western Data:【61†https://support-eu.wd.com/app/answers/detailweb/a_id/34871/~/external-drive%3A-paragon-ntfs-driver-for-mac†support-eu.wd.com】\nL295: \nL296: MacOS\nL297: If you are a MacOS user, it is recommended that you back up your disk files and format the disk to exFAT for use, which can provide a better file compatibility experience.\nL298: \nL299: MassStorage usage problem\nL300: \nL301: You can try it as follows:\nL302: \nL303:   * Make sure the USB cable connection is normal\nL304:   * After confirming that the USB cable is connected, make sure that USB is currently using the USB storage protocol;\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHiBy X1600 R Player | HiBy WiKi (https://guide.hiby.com/en/docs/question/audio_player/x1600_r/)\nciteturn823view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://guide.hiby.com/en/docs/question/audio_player/x1600_r/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 349\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2:   * 【1†Question】\nL3: \nL4:   * 【2†Player】\nL5: \nL6:     * 【2†HiBy Android R Player】\nL7:     * 【3†HiBy X1000 R Player】\nL8:     * 【4†HiBy X1600 R Player】\nL9:     * 【5†HiBy Digital Android M Player】\nL10:   * 【6†USB DAC】\nL11: \nL12:   * 【7†BT DAC】\nL13: \nL14: [Button: On this page]\nL15: \nL16: # HiBy X1600 R Player\nL17: \nL18: ## Description【8†​】\nL19: \nL20: The following applies to the HiBy R Series X1600E chip platform player, including but not limited to the following models: R Series: `HiBy R3PROII`\nL21: `HiBy R3II(2025)`\nL22: `HiBy R1`\nL23: \nL24: ## System【9†​】\nL25: \nL26: * * *\nL27: ### How to upgrade the player?【10†​】\nL28:   * Network upgrade: System settings -》 System upgrade -》 Network upgrade\nL29:   * TF/MicroSD card upgrade (can be turned on): Copy the downloaded upt file to the root directory of the TF/MicroSD card (player connected to the computer), on the player: System Settings -》 System Upgrade -》 TF/MicroSD card upgrade\nL30:   * TF/MicroSD card upgrade (cannot boot): Copy the downloaded upt file to the root directory of the TF/MicroSD card (using a card reader), insert the TF/MicroSD card into the player, press and hold the upgrade key to turn on the computer (the specific models are different), and the machine enters the upgrade interface to upgrade\nL31: * * *\nL32: ### What file formats are supported for TF/MicroSD cards?【11†​】\nL33: \nL34:   * FAT32 、 Exfat 、 NTFS ；\nL35: \nL36: * * *\nL37: \nL38: ### What capacity is the maximum supported TF/MicroSD card?【12†​】\nL39: \nL40:   * TF cards that support up to 2T;\nL41: \nL42: ## Power Supply【13†​】\nL43: \nL44: * * *\nL45: ### Sleep shutdown definition【14†​】\nL46:   * The sleep shutdown timing point is calculated from when the sleep shutdown is turned on;\nL47:   * After the sleep shutdown is turned on, if the setting value is 10 minutes, the formatting and updating the media library are operated within these 10 minutes. The shutdown time will be calculated again and will automatically shut down after 10 minutes.\nL48:   * Restart after sleep shutdown, and save the original set value during sleep time; the sleep switch state is off;\nL49:   * After turning on sleep, restart after manually shutting down the computer, and save the original set value of the sleep time; the sleep switch state is off;\nL50:   * Regardless of whether the charger is plugged in or not, the time will not be recalculated under any conditions after turning on the sleep shutdown time (including the operation of squeezing or adding or decreasing volume, plugging and unplugging the TF card, plugging and unplugging the charger, etc.), and will automatically shut down after the setting value;\nL51:   * When plugging in the charger, set to shut down after sleep (1. When the battery is not full, directly enter the charging interface after shutting down. 2. When the battery is full, turn off the black screen directly);\nL52:   * The computer connects USB and sets to shut down after sleep after shutting down the computer without responding to sleep;\nL53: * * *\nL54: ### Power saving shutdown definition【15†​】\nL55:   * Restart after power saving and power saving shutdown time and power saving switch save the original set value;\nL56:   * The power-saving shutdown timing point is calculated from when the machine stops any operation (any operations include: playing music, adding or decreasing volume, key operation, playing music, etc.);\nL57:   * When plugging in the charger, set the power saving and shutdown (1. When the battery is not full, directly enter the charging interface after shutting down. 2. When the battery is full, the screen will be turned off directly;\nL58:   * The computer connects USB and sets power saving and shutdown after setting power saving and shutdown without responding to power saving;\nL59: ## Audio【16†​】\nL60: \nL61: * * *\nL62: \nL63: ### Album cover supports specifications?【17†​】\nL64: \nL65:   * bmp 2048*2048\nL66:   * jpg Unlimited\nL67:   * png 1024*1024\nL68: \nL69: * * *\nL70: ### External cover specification priority?【18†​】\nL71:   * Built-in cover;\nL72:   * Pictures with the same name as the song in the folder of the song will be displayed in the order of priority in the format;\nL73:   * In the folder of the song, the picture with the prefix of the picture with the \"front\" prefix in front of the picture is displayed in the order of format;\nL74:   * In the folder of the song, the picture with the prefix of the picture with the prefix of \"cover\" is displayed in the order of formatting;\nL75:   * In the folder of the song, the picture with the prefix of the picture with the prefix of \"folder\" is displayed in the order of format;\nL76:   * Supported formats are (\"bmp\", \"jpg\", \"png\")\nL77: * * *\nL78: ### Is there a limit on the number of files?【19†​】\nL79: \nL80:   * The maximum limit for files without CUE is 5800, and when there is CUE, the maximum limit is 6200;\nL81:   * 8000 heads are still relatively easy to cause insufficient memory, so the scope is further narrowed;\nL82: \nL83: * * *\nL84: \nL85: ### Song Meta Information Priority【20†​】\nL86: \nL87:   * ID3V2 > APEV2 > ALL；\nL88:   * Windows can use mp3tag to edit meta information;\nL89: \nL90: * * *\nL91: ### Limit the number of recent playlist files saved【21†​】\nL92: \nL93:   * Only 50 recent songs are saved under the recent playlist;\nL94: \nL95: * * *\nL96: \nL97: ### Description of the limit number of internal playlists【22†​】\nL98: \nL99:   * There is no limit on the number of songs under the new playlist and the list (unlimited)\nL100:   * Unlimited number of my favorites\nL101: \nL102: * * *\nL103: \nL104: ### What music formats does the X1600 platform support?【23†​】\nL105: \nL106:   * mp3\\wav\\flac\\ape\\wma\\ogg\\m4a\\aac\\mp2\\aif\\aiff\nL107:   * dsf\\dff\\iso(normal, non-dst)\nL108: \nL109: * * *\nL110: ### Which music formats does the X1600 platform CUE support?【24†​】\nL111: \nL112:   * ape\\flac\\wav\\m4a\\mp3\nL113: \nL114: * * *\nL115: \nL116: ### Does the X1600 platform support DST music files?【25†​】\nL117: \nL118:   * The X1600 platform does not support ISO (DST) and DST music files\nL119: \nL120: * * *\nL121: ### What are the track order definitions under each list of the X1600 platform?【26†​】\nL122: \nL123:   * All list (including folder track list)/artist/style:\nL124: \nL125:     1. First, sort the numbers in order\nL126:     2. Then the first letter (capital in front) A/B...\nL127:     3. Pinyin first letter\nL128:     4. CUE audio track (Special characters are inserted into the list in the character order of the ascii code table)\nL129:   * Folder list (folder name) sort:\nL130:     1. Number size order\nL131:     2. Letters (capital in front)\nL132:     3. Pinyin first letter (special characters are inserted into the list in the character order of the ascii code table)\nL133:   * Album title sort:\nL134: \nL135:     1. Sort by album ID first\nL136:     2. If there is no album ID, sort it in order of numerical size\nL137:     3. The first letter (capital in front) A/B...\nL138:     4. Pinyin first letter\nL139:   * Artist name and style name sort:\nL140:     1. First, sort the numbers in order\nL141:     2. Then the first letter (capital in front) A/B...\nL142:     3. Pinyin first letter\nL143: \nL144: * * *\nL145: ### What should I do if there are garbled words in songs and lyrics? Solution and reasons?【27†​】\nL146: \nL147:   * The inconsistency between the song information encoding and the selected language leads to errors in string parsing;\nL148:   * The solution is that the user re-edits the song information or lyrics information with mp3tag and uses local encoding to save the song information;\nL149: \nL150: * * *\nL151: #### What do the error prompts -5, -9, -11, -12, -13 mean?【28†​】\nL152:   * SAE_USED -5, there is an exception in the code part. You can contact the developer (ringsd@hiby.com)\nL153:   * SAE_FORMAT -9, file format error\nL154:   * SAE_RANGE -11, file sampling rate exceeds the support range\nL155:   * SAE_NOTSUPPORT-12, file not supported\nL156:   * SAE_IO -13, possible reasons: the file format does not support (maybe some features), the file is corrupted, the file is encrypted, the output device does not support (USB DAC or BT playback), network exceptions (communication exceptions with the server side, such as: FM, TIDAL, Qobuz), etc., or other types of problems can be fed back to customer service.\nL157: * * *\nL158: ### Under what circumstances will seamless play take effect?【29†​】\nL159: \nL160:   * Seamless playback is mainly used to solve the problem that the original single-track file is cut into multiple split files, resulting in song pauses or gaps in the middle;\nL161:   * PCM type music supports seamlessness, but the same samples must be switched to achieve seamless effects;\nL162:   * The seamless play option is invalid for DSD format;\nL163: \nL164: * * *\nL165: ### Does Bluetooth car support song information?【30†​】\nL166: \nL167:   * As the transmitting end, the X1600 platform player will send song title, artist, album, style, duration, current playback progress and other information. If the receiving end processes the data on the transmitting end, it can be displayed normally. If there is no processing, then it will not be displayed;\nL168:   * Lyric display is not supported;\nL169: \nL170: * * *\nL171: ### Does EQ (equalizer) support range for sampling rate?【31†​】\nL172: \nL173:   * PCM supports up to 48kHz;\nL174:   * DSD/MQA songs are not supported;\nL175:   * Both digital output and analog output are effective;\nL176: \nL177: * * *\nL178: \nL179: ### MSEB supports range for sampling rate?【32†​】\nL180: \nL181:   * PCM supports up to 384kHz;\nL182:   * DSD/MQA songs are not supported;\nL183:   * Both digital output and analog output are effective;\nL184: \nL185: * * *\nL186: ### DSD output mode (PCM\\DoP\\Native) function definition【33†​】\nL187:   * When the audio output is balanced (BAL)\\single-ended (PO)\\LineOut(LO), the DSD output mode selection cannot be performed. This function is invalid and output according to the actual situation;\nL188:   * When the audio output is Bluetooth, DSD output mode cannot be selected. This function is invalid and is converted to PCM output;\nL189:   * When the audio output is SPDIF, DSD output mode selection can be performed Select PCM and output according to the sampling rate 88.2kHz Select DoP or Native. When playing DSD64, it will be output in DoP mode, and the output sampling rate is 2.8 MHz; Select DoP or Native. When playing DSD128 and DSD256, they are converted to PCM output, with an output sampling rate of 88.2 kHz.\nL190:   * When the audio output is USB Audio Out, DSD output mode selection can be performed Select PCM, press PCM to output, the maximum support sampling rate is 384kHz Select DoP, press DoP to output, and support DSD256 (11.2MHz) at most (condition: The decoding device that needs to be accessed supports DSD decoding.\nL191: If the device does not support it, the playback fails) Select Native, press Native to output, and support DSD256 (11.2MHz) at most (condition: The decoding and decoding device that needs to be accessed supports DSD decoding. If the device does not support it, the playback fails)\nL192: * * *\nL193: ### Qobuz reported 400 error?【34†​】\nL194: \nL195:   * Time Setting Set the correct date and 24 hours time system\nL196: \nL197: * * *\nL198: \nL199: ### USB DAC input Bluetooth output, matter description:【35†​】\nL200: \nL201:   * The sampling rate support is limited by Bluetooth output coding: Bluetooth SBC coding (44.1/48) LDAC coding (44.1/48/88.2/96). (If it exceeds it, there will be no output)\nL202: \nL203: ## Other【36†​】\nL204: \nL205: * * *\nL206: ### How to set up and add dynamic covers?【37†​】\nL207: \nL208:   * Settings portal: System Settings -》 Screensaver Settings -》 Dynamic Cover\nL209:   * Create a new screensavers folder in the MicroSD(TF) directory (casing is required for directory) and place the picture in this directory:\nL210:     1. Support jpg and png formats\nL211:     2. The resolution is recommended not to exceed 1024*1024\nL212:     3. The number of pictures should not exceed 16\nL213: \nL214: * * *\nL215: ### How to add a custom network station?【38†​】\nL216: \nL217: Method 1\nL218: \nL219:   * Create new text with UTF8 encoding in the root directory of MicroSD(TF) card`radio.txt`\nL220:   * Open text Fill in the station name and URL, name and URL in English characters`,`Split, one radio station per line, for example:\nL221: \nL222:     `中国之声, http://ngcdn001.cnr.cn/live/zgzs/index.m3u8\nL223:     音乐之声, http://ngcdn003.cnr.cn/live/yyzs/index.m3u8\nL224:     `\nL225: \nL226: Method 2\nL227: \nL228: Thanks to the reddit user`dough10`, original connection reference【39†Refer†www.reddit.com】\nL229:   1. Select the desired radio station on (【40†https://customradio.dough10.me†customradio.dough10.me】)\nL230:   2. Click Download, and the browser will download a radio.txt file\nL231:   3. Copy radio.txt to the MicroSD(TF) card root directory. If there are already some radio stations, you can append the downloaded radio.txt content to the original radio.txt)\nL232: \nL233: * * *\n"
